-- The component catalogue pipeline shipped (20260930000001_component_catalog.sql,
-- scripts/scrape-signaturesolar-catalog.ts, scripts/import-cec-pv.ts), and the
-- operator split the rest of the request into two sessions: the admin
-- catalogue that verifies and publishes, and the calculator pickers that read
-- what it publishes. Both go on the board here so neither lives only in a chat.
--
-- Keep in sync with supabase/seed.sql (CI enforces this).

update public.roadmap_items
  set status = 'in_development',
      dev_percent_complete = 75,
      description = 'Started 2026-08-30. src/lib/inverter-sizing.ts and src/lib/pv-string.ts carry INVERTER_PRESETS and PANEL_PRESETS behind an admission gate: every figure from the manufacturer''s own datasheet, opened and read, with a reachable sourceUrl and the matching review module passing. Four rows so far. Inverters: Sun Gold Power SPH8048P and SPH10048P (manufacturer manual V1.3 pp.58-59) and the EG4 6000XP (spec sheet VER 1.4.4). The EG4 row is the argument for the gate — refused on first attempt because its window and per-tracker current came from a search index, and when the PDF itself became readable the search figure proved WRONG: 25 A is the MAX. SHORT CIRCUIT INPUT CURRENT while MAX. USABLE INPUT CURRENT is 17 A. Recording 25 A as usable would have permitted about half again as many parallel strings as the tracker can convert, silently. That correction closed a modelling gap: pv-string.ts now treats current as a pair exactly like voltage, damage limit versus harvest limit. Panels: the Sun Gold Power SG550WM, from the manufacturer''s own datasheet served off their Shopify CDN (their own store, so it is their document, not a reseller''s transcription). A useful first row because it publishes all three fields the string maths needs and many sheets omit: the Voc coefficient, the Pmax coefficient, and the 25 A maximum series fuse rating. Its -0.35%/degC Voc coefficient is at the steep end, so it gains more voltage in cold than most: at a -25.9 degC design low it reaches 58.5 V against a 49.7 V label, taking eight in series on a 500 V input where the STC figure suggests ten. src/lib/panel-review.ts is the physics-shaped check, sibling of inverter-review.ts: Vmp below Voc, Imp below Isc, Pmax equal to Vmp x Imp within 5%, fill factor between 0.6 and 0.9, a NEGATIVE Voc coefficient, and a series fuse rating above Isc. SCRAPER FINDING, 2026-08-30: sungoldpower.com product pages DO carry an HTML spec table, but it omits the temperature coefficients and the maximum series fuse rating entirely — exactly the two protection-critical fields. An HTML-only scraper would emit rows that look complete and cannot size a string, silently falling back to a coefficient that understates cold Voc. A panel scraper therefore needs PDF text extraction, which is a devDependency decision not yet taken. Their product pages also cover more than one model in a single table (the 550 W page carries the SG450WM alongside it), so column selection needs the same hand verification the battery scrapers documented. Remaining: that scraper, Supabase panel_models and inverter_models tables with RLS, and /admin review queues mirroring /admin/batteries. Typing a datasheet in by hand stays a first-class path. SCRAPING RESEARCH, 2026-09-10 -- the decisions this item has been blocked on, taken. THE PDF-EXTRACTION DEPENDENCY IS DECIDABLE NOW: use unpdf or pdfjs-dist. Both are pure JavaScript with no native build step, which matters twice -- Homebrew is broken on the development machine, and production is going to Cloudflare, where a native binding would not run. pdf-parse wraps an old pdf.js and is less maintained. That single choice unblocks BOTH the panel and inverter scrapers, which is the whole remaining blocker recorded above. THE CEC SOLAR EQUIPMENT LIST IS THE LARGER UNLOCK, verified 2026-09-10: the California Energy Commission publishes a downloadable PV module list whose Full Data version carries reported nameplate ratings AND TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENTS across 300+ manufacturers -- exactly the fields the sungoldpower HTML table was found to omit. One file instead of N per-brand parsers. BUT IT COLLIDES WITH THE ADMISSION GATE, and that is a decision for the operator rather than a detail: the gate says every figure comes from the manufacturer''s OWN datasheet, opened and read, and a regulator''s list is a transcription. The argument for accepting it is that it is a STRONGER transcription than a reseller''s -- it is the value the manufacturer legally filed. PROPOSED MIDDLE PATH that keeps the gate intact: CEC rows land as unpublished candidates tagged source_tier = ''regulator'', and PUBLISHING still requires opening the manufacturer PDF. That buys a 300-brand candidate pool for free without weakening anything. NOT YET VERIFIED, do not plan around it: whether the Full Data list includes DIMENSIONS AND WEIGHT. Ratings and coefficients are confirmed; the physical fields are not. ALSO WORTH DOING: extend scrape-signaturesolar.ts and scrape-a1solarstore.ts to panels -- both scrapers already exist and are trusted, and both retailers sell panels, which is far cheaper than admitting a new brand. Santan Solar is worth a look for used and B-stock modules, the real DIY price floor, pending robots.txt and whether an affiliate programme exists. Renogy stays excluded on the existing robots.txt precedent. Freight class should be DERIVED from the longest dimension rather than scraped. AND ONE STRUCTURAL FINDING that belongs here: PanelSpec is electrical-only -- ten fields, all volts and amps. A panel_models table must carry the physical fields from the start or it will reproduce the same gap in the database that the TypeScript preset has today. See "Panel choice: form factor, granularity and the physical fields". THE PIPELINE SHIPPED 2026-09-30, and the CEC decision was taken by the operator the same day: the list is a CANDIDATE source, never a verified one. SCHEMA (supabase/migrations/20260930000001_component_catalog.sql): not panel_models and inverter_models as planned above, but one shared component_models table plus typed panel_specs and inverter_specs, so the gate, the listing logic and the admin catalogue are written once, and battery_models can move in later as a third category. MODELS AND LISTINGS ARE SEPARATE: a listing is one shop''s product page keyed on the retailer''s own product id, never the URL, with pack_qty so a pallet of 36 is a second listing of the same panel rather than a second panel -- the forking failure the battery scraper had. THE ADMISSION GATE MOVED INTO THE DATABASE: a spec row records spec_source (cec, retailer, datasheet) and verified_at, a CHECK makes verified mean every protection-register field is present and sourced from the datasheet, and a trigger refuses is_published on any model whose specs are unverified. Scrapers never write a verified spec row. PRICES: written in place and dated on every run, except a move of more than 30% on a published model, which is held beside the live price for an admin (src/lib/listing-price.ts) -- a deliberate departure from the battery proposal queue, because a proposal per listing per week is the queue nobody reads. THE CEC FINDING THAT JUSTIFIES THE GATE: the list is lab-tested data, not a transcription, and for the one panel verified here by hand it DISAGREES with the datasheet in the unsafe direction -- SG550WM beta-Voc -0.259 %/degC on the CEC list, -0.35 on the manufacturer sheet (re-read 2026-09-30), the CEC figure shared by the whole SG*WM family. The shallower figure predicts less cold voltage and permits more panels in series. CEC ANSWERS: dimensions exist for about a quarter of rows and for NONE listed since 2023; there is no weight column. Physical fields therefore come from retailers and datasheets. The list is imported whole into cec_pv_modules as reference data (22,150 modules, 287 manufacturers) rather than as 22k review candidates nobody can buy; the scraper looks each retailer panel up by part number. Trap: retailers often give a family part number (Trina TSM-NE09RC.05) where CEC puts the wattage inside it (TSM-420NE09RC.05). FIRST SOURCE: Signature Solar, discovered from its panel and inverter categories (scripts/scrape-signaturesolar-catalog.ts, weekly via .github/workflows/scrape-catalog.yml at the 10 s crawl delay). First run: 106 listings on 82 models (26 panel models, 56 inverter models); 16 of the 26 panel models pre-filled with CEC electricals. Nothing is published. Signature Solar files trunk cables, end caps and gateways under its microinverter categories, so an inverter must also be named as a unit in its title; three-phase commercial units are skipped. REMAINING: inverter specs arrive empty (shop pages carry only spec-sheet links, and the CEC inverter list covers grid-support units only, not the EG4 6000XP or SPH8048P), so every inverter is filled at review. The review screen is "Admin: component catalogue"; the calculators reading from it are "Calculators: pick real components from the catalogue". A1 SolarStore panels and PDF datasheet reading (unpdf) are next for the pipeline itself.'
  where title = 'Component spec library: inverters and panels';

insert into public.roadmap_items
  (phase, category, title, description, status, dev_percent_complete, is_public, display_order)
values
  (1, 'admin', 'Admin: component catalogue — browse, verify, publish',
   'Session 2 of the component-catalogue work, planned 2026-09-30. The scraper now fills component_models, component_listings, panel_specs and inverter_specs, and NOTHING can be published until an admin verifies a spec row against the manufacturer datasheet -- the database refuses it. So this screen is the only way the new catalogue reaches anyone. ONE CATALOGUE, NOT ONE PAGE PER CATEGORY: /admin/catalog lists every model across panels and inverters (batteries once they move in), filterable by category, brand, published state, spec state (verified / CEC candidate / no electricals) and held prices, searchable by brand, model and part number, sortable by price and $/W. Pallet listings show a per-panel price. MODEL PAGE: photo, every listing with its retailer, pack size, minimum order and dated price; the spec row side by side with its CEC row and the spec-sheet link; a VERIFY form that records the datasheet figures and runs panel-review.ts / inverter-review.ts before setting verified_at; then Publish. Accept or discard a held price. HIDE, DON''T DELETE: deleting a scraped row lets next week''s run re-insert it -- the battery duplicate problem -- so rejecting needs a hidden state the scraper respects. MERGE two models that turned out to be one product (a listing whose part number differed from its sibling''s). ALSO: move battery_models into the catalogue as a third category, retiring /admin/batteries into it; seed the four hand-verified PANEL_PRESETS and INVERTER_PRESETS rows as datasheet-verified models so the calculators have something published on day one.',
   'planned', 0, false, 106),

  (1, 'calculators', 'Calculators: pick real components from the catalogue',
   'Session 3 of the component-catalogue work, planned 2026-09-30, and the user-facing half of it: an easier way to choose a panel and an inverter from what ZonZelf already has, instead of typing a datasheet in. Depends on "Admin: component catalogue" -- only published, datasheet-verified models are offered. The inverter step and the strings step read published component_models and their specs instead of the INVERTER_PRESETS and PANEL_PRESETS arrays. A searchable picker, narrowed to what fits the design so far: inverters by battery voltage and continuous watts, panels that wire into whole strings inside the chosen tracker''s window at the site''s design low. Physical fields feed the form-factor work in "Panel choice: form factor, granularity and the physical fields". Each pick shows its retailer listings with a dated price and the affiliate disclosure (src/lib/affiliate.ts), per-panel price for pallets, and the minimum order. Typing a datasheet in by hand stays a first-class path. Differentiator #1 -- a beginner picks a real product and sees why it fits -- and the affiliate line for the two largest purchases after the battery.',
   'planned', 0, true, 107);
